import json
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).parent;O=R/'output';O.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
BASE=R.parent/'v3.0.0' if (R.parent/'v3.0.0/World_Atlas.json').exists() else R/'source'
w=json.loads((BASE/'World_Atlas.json').read_text());g=json.loads((BASE/'Blueprint_Data.json').read_text())
def rec(id,room,cast,title,history,daily,conflict,scene,props,materials):
 b=next(x for x in g['signature_building_plans'] if x['id']==id);c=next(x for x in w['communities'] if x['id']==b['settlement'])
 return {'id':'INT-'+id,'building_id':id,'name':b['name'],'community':c['name'],'community_id':c['id'],'focus_room':room,'cast':cast,'chapter_title':title,'history':history,'daily_life':daily,'conflict':conflict,'scene_seed':scene,'signature_props':props,'material_direction':materials,'geometry':b,'authority':'New additive interior dressing and local history. Existing footprint, walls, doors, levels, geography and established character history remain unchanged.'}
records=[
rec('EXT-01-B01','Common room',['KIN-01','KIN-02','VIL-01'],'The line above the door',
'The kitchen began as a ferry-workers meal fund, long after Reedbank itself was founded. Households contributed labor as often as money. The building survived several ordinary floods because its raised floor was maintained, not because the river spared deserving people. Seven years before the pilot, its dry rooms became temporary shelter. Afterward, the volunteers copied the flood height onto a removable measuring board: the room itself was not flooded to that height. Some families remember generous depot deliveries; others remember the prices that followed. Both memories are true. Dara keeps the serving rota beside the donations ledger so a wealthy name cannot erase a month of unpaid work.',
'Before dawn, kitchen workers arrive through the normal entrance and carry ingredients along the passage. Breakfast is served from a movable cart in the common room; cooking remains in the separate kitchen. Upper guest rooms are occupied by travelers and visiting relatives. At midday, children copy recipes while adults repair river baskets. A red token means a bowl has already been paid for; nobody receiving it must explain why. The dry measuring board is brought out only for Watermark Night.',
'Veyra offers to replace the worn tables if her depot receives exclusive supply rights. Refusing leaves a real expense; accepting would weaken the kitchen’s independence. Dara favors a slower public subscription. Hollis worries that pride can also become an excuse to let people go hungry. Their disagreement is affectionate, practical and unresolved until the actual bids are compared.',
'At Watermark Night, a child paints a flood line too high. Hollis starts to correct it, then asks whose memory the child followed. The answer belongs to a house on a lower wharf. The kitchen adds the house datum to the board instead of declaring one family wrong. Madger learns that a shared record must preserve where each witness stood.',
['Long serving table','Red-token bowl','Removable flood board','Repaired river basket'],
'Raised timber floor, pale lime plaster, dark beams, ochre ceramics, blue-gray river light and warm task lamps. No water or river channel inside the common room.'),
rec('EXT-02-B02','Bench room',['GORILLA_WHITE','NEW-13','ALLY-02'],'The unfinished note',
'The White family workshop belongs to the working terrace, not to a heroic dynasty. Its benches have been shortened, widened and patched for successive users. Nine years before the pilot, the platform failure injured Eris and exposed Gorilla’s refusal to call a halt. The failed platform was at a worksite, not secretly beneath this floor. The workshop later adopted a stop-board beside its tool checkout: any worker can suspend a task and record a reason. Eris recovered, adapted her methods and became a crew lead. Her brother’s guilt is only one part of her life; she also competes in terrible music contests and dislikes visitors who turn every conversation into an inquiry about her hand.',
'Stone fitting is prepared in the bench room and assembled across the passage. The tool store closes at shift end, while the wash/office remains available for handover notes. Dust collection is a maintained work practice, never a magical absence of dust. Meals happen away from the cutting benches. A half-built percussion frame is a voluntary evening project, and nobody is allowed to conscript an apprentice into finishing it.',
'A donor wants Gorilla’s name placed above the workshop door. Eris wants the money for better shared equipment but refuses to make the place a monument to someone who lives elsewhere. Oren believes the practical benefit matters more than the sign. Gorilla must ask what help is wanted and accept an answer smaller than a grand rescue.',
'Gorilla offers to carry a loaded workbench alone. Eris asks him to hold a measuring cord while an apprentice marks the floor. He can do it perfectly, yet feels useless. The scene turns when he notices the apprentice is afraid to question a measurement and invites the objection before Eris has to.',
['Stop-board easel','Percussion-frame jig','Paired fitting bench','Repaired measuring cord'],
'Cut limestone walls, dark timber, copper roof weathering, pale stone dust, rust-red work cloths. Warm side light; no forge or open furnace in this household workshop.'),
rec('EXT-03-B02','Common room',['ALLY-01','NEW-12','SNOW_WHITE'],'The empty chair',
'The Six Roads name is a supper custom, not evidence that six new highways enter Latchford. The town grew around three roads; the inn later named six tables for six common onward destinations. During the Severance, households took turns hosting stranded travelers, and the custom outlived the emergency. A generation ago the inn introduced a departure book with corrections written visibly beneath the original entry. This made it less elegant and more useful. Asha cooked for convoys here before retirement. Sela still trusts the room, but her concealed evacuation funding has not yet become public at the pilot baseline.',
'Arrivals leave wet gear on freestanding racks before entering the common room. Supper seats people by destination. Sleeping quarters remain private; a meal token does not purchase access to a guest’s route notes. Dispatch work belongs to the convoy house and relay offices elsewhere. Snow’s familiar resting mat can be placed beneath the common-room side table when Asha visits. It is removed and stored after her stay, not permanently installed as a shrine.',
'An innkeeper can protect a traveler’s privacy while still warning about an unsafe route. Sela sometimes confuses useful discretion with the secrecy that protected her own decision. Asha notices the distinction but will not expose another person’s history as dinner entertainment. The room allows a confrontation to begin with a practical question rather than an accusation.',
'One place remains set for a delayed traveler. Sela offers to buy the unused meal. Asha refuses to call it unused until the route steward checks the arrival yard. Snow hears a familiar harness buckle before anyone sees its owner. The delay proves ordinary, but the argument reveals which characters assume absence means abandonment.',
['Destination table','Correction ledger stand','Travel-gear rack','Removable canine rest mat'],
'Warm timber arcades, blue convoy cloth, terracotta cups, rain-dark stone at the threshold. A hospitable rectangular room; no new tunnel, hidden hatch or indoor road.'),
rec('EXT-04-B01','Assembly',['MADGER_BOT','NEW-11','VIL-04'],'The machine that may decline',
'Varr Workshop grew from a cooperative making canal instruments and repair tools. Eight years before the pilot, its members built Bot’s body and supported the sustained learning through which he became a person. The workshop owns its spare components and drawings; it does not own him. His departure for the Burrow five years before the pilot was a choice. Lume saved an early maintenance worksheet because she is sentimental, not because it contains a secret override. The canal flood that shaped Nix’s distrust affected a different part of Millmere’s civic history. Bot’s origin and Nix’s grievance overlap in place without becoming one hidden master plot.',
'Morning work begins with tool calibration in the bench room. Projects enter the assembly room only after their owners agree to the scope of repair. The office keeps consent and change records; private notes are not posted on the public job board. A practice instrument occupies one rolling stand. It is mechanically ordinary and often out of tune. Visitors can sit in the assembly room without being measured, scanned or assigned work.',
'Lume offers Bot a diagnostic check as a gesture of care. Bot wants to visit as a guest. Her hurt is understandable, but it creates no right of access. Their conflict resolves through an agreed narrow task and an evening spent doing something neither can optimize. Nix could exploit the workshop’s reputation, but no magical maker command or ownership claim becomes valid.',
'Bot asks to borrow a tool and Lume reaches for it before asking which one. He waits. She notices, puts it back and lets him describe his own project. His attempt at music produces an ugly buzzing note. Lume laughs only after he does, and the awkward room becomes a shared workshop again.',
['Consent job-board','Isolated repair bench','Rolling instrument stand','Early worksheet case'],
'Brick, dark timber, brass gauges, painted enamel, canal-reflected light. Friendly practical machinery with physical switches; no operating theater, restraining chair or master control console.'),
rec('EXT-05-B01','West stalls',['RES-04','RES-08'],'A seed is not a promise',
'The Seed House developed from Willowmere’s seasonal exchange, after the orchard settlement was already established. Growers first traded surplus in cloth packets; later they recorded harvest conditions on ceramic tags that survived damp storage. A rare seed without its growing history became less valuable than an ordinary seed with a reliable account. The hall’s stalls still belong to rotating contributors, not hereditary vendors. Neri remembers learning here; Sori remembers the labor behind the display. Neither memory makes one of them the rightful owner of the place.',
'The central public aisle stays open from end to end. West stalls display dry seeds and growing notes; east stalls hold demonstration trays and tools. Live trials are tended in the existing community gardens rather than turning the hall into an unrecorded greenhouse. Children make impossible plants in clay during the exchange. Bad harvests are entered alongside successes, and a failed packet may remain on display with its explanation.',
'A merchant wants the most spectacular canopy seed displayed without the warning that it rarely grows outside its native conditions. Sori fears losing the sale; Neri fears teaching a convenient lie. They need an honest demonstration that supports the growers instead of treating commerce and learning as automatic enemies.',
'A child asks why the ugliest clay plant has the longest label. Neri reads its imagined needs; Sori asks who would do that work. The class redesigns the plant together. The joke becomes a practical lesson in how a community supports something beautiful.',
['Seed-history cabinet','Ceramic provenance tags','Trial-results table','Clay impossible-plant tray'],
'Orchard timber, cream plaster, woven green shades, shallow ceramic drawers, filtered morning sunlight. All stalls remain inside the inherited hall; central aisle unobstructed.'),
rec('EXT-06-B01','Common room',['VIL-08','NEW-06','IND-01'],'The last whistle is not the end',
'The hostel stands in the occupied worker district of Cinder Cuts. The freight diversion eighteen years before the pilot reduced paid shifts, but it did not erase the residents or their skills. Families converted short-stay rooms into a mixture of temporary lodging and stable tenancy. Raiders arrived seven years before the pilot and occupied the disused eastern spur, not the entire town. The hostel’s patched furniture records years of ordinary repair. Dreg has relatives in the legitimate neighborhood; this gives him a reason to change, not an excuse for the harm he helps cause.',
'At dawn, workers post available hauling and repair jobs. The kitchen serves tenants before taking outside orders. A common-room timetable lists legitimate freight and walking connections; it does not advertise raider patrols. Guests can join the Last Whistle percussion rehearsal using retired, cleaned components. No visitor is required to disclose family ties to stay. Threats are reported through residents’ chosen representatives.',
'Brakka claims that only her crew provides protection. Residents remember help from some crew members and extortion from others. Kesh must acknowledge that Kettle Crown benefited from the diversion while refusing Brakka’s claim to speak for everyone. Pip can arrange a useful delivery but must reveal his commission before the hostel accepts it.',
'Dreg sees a food crate marked for the hostel among stolen freight. He initially wants to return only that crate. A relative asks why the other families’ names matter less. The scene begins his conflict; it does not erase the need to return goods and testify later.',
['Public work board','Repaired communal table','Retired whistle stand','Labeled food-crate rack'],
'Basalt, limewashed repairs, salvaged timber, patched amber lamps and clean kitchen ceramics. Occupied, cared-for rooms; poverty is not rendered as universal filth or villain decoration.'),
rec('EXT-07-B02','Assembly',['NEW-01','GORILLA_WHITE'],'A bridge that listens',
'Crownrest’s bridgewright practice grew alongside a settlement that moves with living crowns. Early builders tried to keep every connection taut; damage taught later crews to allow measured movement. The pavilion keeps deliberately different sample joints so apprentices can compare their behavior. Its spring position is the registered atlas baseline. Seasonal displacement changes recorded site state rather than silently changing the town map. Senn’s knowledge of a protected route belongs to relationships beyond the workshop; no conveniently hidden map sits in a public drawer.',
'Crews check lashings and inspect sample joints before leaving for the bridges. The assembly room builds replaceable sections; full spans are not magically unfolded inside a small room. Dry fiber stays in the tool store. The office records which crown positions a measurement belongs to. A warning chime is a local work signal, not a universal command over trees. Normal conversation and joking continue between inspections.',
'Festival organizers want a crossing reopened before its movement settles. Senn knows delaying it will cost traders money. Gorilla initially offers more strength and more rope; Senn needs him to keep the waiting crowd from pressuring the inspection crew. A successful defense of the community can mean protecting a careful decision.',
'A child hangs a bell on a sample joint, making every small movement audible. Senn intends to remove it as a distraction, then hears the interval change before he sees the lashing bind. The bell is retained as a teaching aid, with a note that sound alone cannot certify a bridge.',
['Flexible-joint test frame','Dry fiber cabinet','Seasonal position board','Teaching bell stand'],
'Living-fiber lashings, dark timber, pale resin repairs, green reflected canopy light. Ordinary interior floor and inherited walls; no giant trunk suddenly occupying the central passage.'),
rec('EXT-08-B02','Public reading',['NEW-02','NEW-08'],'The honest shadow',
'Ossara’s observatory predates the Severance, but survival did not preserve a perfect archive. Some pages were recopied, some instruments were replaced and some convenient conclusions became tradition. The present public reading room exhibits a damaged measuring plate beside its corrected replacement. Maela learned to value that admission. Kavar sponsors attractive public demonstrations because they build trust; his generosity does not make his water claims accurate. The observatory is not the source of the city’s water and contains no secret aquifer switch.',
'Morning readers compare weather notes before the hottest hours. Demonstrations use covered lamps and small mirrors on a screened table; no concentrated beam crosses the public passage. Observing equipment is stored between sessions. The dispatch room schedules public access and communicates findings, while the upper catalogs and ground archives retain their registered uses. Curtains reduce glare without making the room gloomy.',
'A reflected mark appears to prove that a caravan missed a payment. Maela can explain the optical error, but doing so challenges a respected sponsor. She needs original measurements and witnesses rather than a heroic speech about truth. Kavar can concede one faulty display while continuing to defend the larger debt system.',
'Two visitors stand in different places and insist that a marked disc points in opposite directions. Maela swaps their positions before explaining the reflection. The audience sees the problem before the argument about a disputed record begins.',
['Screened mirror demonstration','Comparison ledger desk','Damaged measuring-plate case','Covered instrument cabinet'],
'Pale sandstone, turquoise shutters, cream linen, brass and dark glass. Warm bounced desert light, controlled bright reflections, no lasers or glowing magical debt machine.'),
rec('EXT-09-B02','Shelter west',['NEW-03','RES-07'],'What the tide returns',
'The rescue house was established by harbor crews whose families had disagreed over salvage rights for generations. They agreed that rescue should not transfer ownership of a rescued person’s boat or cargo. That principle is posted with actual examples of difficult claims, not a flawless founding legend. The current structure stands on the registered upper refuge datum. The older drowned district and archive are separate places. Toma knows the rescue rule well; his temptation lies in deciding which objects count as abandoned once their owners are hard to find.',
'Wet arrivals are assessed in the western shelter. Dry supplies remain in the rear store; gear is cleaned in the guide/wash room before reuse. The eastern shelter gives families a quieter waiting space. Boats moor outside the building’s inherited footprint. A tide board records the last observed level and who measured it, so a copied number is never mistaken for a fresh observation.',
'A recovered chest contains saleable fittings and papers belonging to a living descendant community. Toma can pay his crew quickly by selling the fittings, but removing them could destroy the evidence that establishes the chest’s origin. Edda insists that care for the rescued crew comes before the ownership hearing; she does not claim authority to settle the hearing herself.',
'A rescued rival tries to repay Toma with a family token. He almost takes it, then asks whether the offer would still be made after a warm meal and a night’s sleep. The pause changes how the crew sees him more than a speech about generosity would.',
['Dry assessment bench','Observed-tide board','Rescue-kit rack','Recovered-property tray'],
'Limestone, salt-worn blue timber, coral-colored plaster, rope baskets and strong daylight. Dry refuge interior above the harbor, no wave washing through the room.'),
rec('EXT-10-B02','Assembly',['NEW-04','NEW-09'],'The cost of a light thing',
'Aurel Quay’s first charter rejected the sale of residents with vessels. Later dock contracts found subtler ways to trap labor through debt and supposedly temporary rescue fees. Sailmakers Court became a place where crews compared those terms alongside their repair work. Its cooperative did not invent liftstone and cannot suspend a ship through willpower. Eri’s expertise is practical: sail condition, weather, loads and the courage to refuse a profitable departure. Serik knows the same craft well enough to disguise exploitation as exceptional service.',
'The assembly room holds short sail sections and test frames, not a full ship. Pattern work happens in the bench room; the tool store keeps dry cloth and fittings. Loads are weighed on an ordinary floor scale. Wind checks are taken outside at the existing dock and copied to the office with a time. The hall has solid floors and walls; the cloud sea is visible only through appropriate openings.',
'A contract promises that rescue charges will end after the next profitable trip, but gives the rescuer control over which trips are allowed. Eri sees the trap. Serik challenges her to offer an immediate alternative. The community must share limited capacity and accept delayed freight, making solidarity a costly choice rather than an easy slogan.',
'During Weight Day, a toy boat wins by carrying less cargo than its label claims. The children laugh until Eri asks what happens when a real captain makes the same substitution. The toy is rebuilt, and its second run is slower but honest.',
['Sail-section test frame','Mechanical load scale','Contract comparison desk','Weight Day toy cradle'],
'Basalt floor, ochre canvas, copper fittings, indigo shadows and high cloud light. Interior scale is modest; no vessel floating inside and no floor opening onto an unguarded abyss.'),
rec('EXT-11-B02','Public reading',['NEW-05','NEW-10','MADGER_BOT'],'A memory needs a witness',
'Hushvale’s archive inherited records of both necessary emergency closures and later abuses of that authority. During the Severance ninety-two years before the pilot, cutting some resonance links prevented a cascade. That true act of protection became a powerful story, and powerful stories attract convenient additions. Echo ice preserves fragments under limited conditions; it does not provide an omniscient recording of history. Ysolde’s training includes identifying gaps. Ossa’s fear is that public uncertainty will reopen a danger she believes the archive alone understands.',
'The public room contains ordinary copies and supervised demonstrations. Sensitive originals remain in their registered archive rooms. A cool cabinet protects a small demonstration fragment; the reading room itself is habitable and warm. No one heats an original for a dramatic effect. Visitors can compare transcriptions, including disagreements and uncertainty marks. A demonstration’s limits are explained before a voice is played.',
'A true warning is attached to a forged continuing prohibition. Rejecting the forgery does not prove the old danger was imaginary. Ysolde needs to separate the claims while Ossa treats separation as disloyalty. Bot must preserve uncertainty and decline a request to infer missing words simply because the crowd wants a complete sentence.',
'An echo repeats a tender phrase with the same breath twice. A visitor hears a loved one; Ysolde hears a copied fragment. She asks permission before explaining the flaw. Respect for grief becomes part of accurate interpretation.',
['Paired transcription desk','Insulated fragment cabinet','Uncertainty-mark display','Supervised listening seat'],
'Limestone, insulated copper fittings, dark wool, blue glacier reflection and warm reading lamps. Small contained ice specimen only; no vast ice wall replacing a room partition.'),
rec('EXT-12-B01','Bench room',['NEW-06','VIL-08'],'The cup with two handles',
'The Common Kiln Hall coordinates making, inspection and exchange. High-temperature firing takes place in its separately registered kiln annex, not beneath occupied rooms. Kettle Crown prospered after the freight diversion that hurt Cinder Cuts eighteen years before the pilot. Individual makers did not all choose the policy, yet they inherited its advantages. The hall’s public defect shelf began as a way to teach apprentices; it later became a place where makers admitted rushed orders and bad purchasing decisions as well as cracks in clay.',
'Greenware is inspected in the bench room before being moved along the clear passage toward its next handling stage. The assembly room packs cooled work. The tool store separates clean supplies from used molds. The wash/office records firing batches and cooperative orders. Cups displayed at the Cooling Feast are fully cooled; nobody handles glowing ceramics as a harmless gag.',
'A relief order for Cinder Cuts could help residents and also be used as publicity that erases the harm of the diversion. Kesh wants public accounts, fair freight terms and a named residents’ contact. Dreg wants a shortcut that would let his crew take credit. Their negotiation reveals whether he is ready to help without controlling the story.',
'Two makers submit a cup with handles on opposite sides as an absurd festival entry. A resident with a recovering wrist finds it useful. The makers have to decide whether they care more about winning the joke prize or letting someone change the purpose of their work.',
['Greenware inspection bench','Cooled defect shelf','Two-handled cup cradle','Batch record stand'],
'Dark volcanic stone, white clay dust, red ceramics, green garden reflections. Calm cool workroom; the separate hot annex may be implied beyond the exterior, never moved indoors.'),
rec('EXT-13-B01','Public aisle',['NEW-07','MADGER','NEW-03'],'The map that stays home',
'Lumen Tide’s harbor traditions are older than the surface Open Ways and preserve more than one founding account. The Tide Speakers Hall developed as a meeting place among communities with different routes, harvest practices and memories of migration. Its public chart gives travelers useful bearings while withholding nesting-water detail entrusted to particular stewards. This is a living civic practice, not a puzzle whose moral reward is letting a surface hero take the complete map. Namu’s temporary role as a speaker does not make them ruler of the deep city.',
'The central aisle serves public circulation and meetings; movable benches are brought from the side bays for a hearing and returned afterward. No fixed dais blocks the north-south route. Fungal produce arrives from the existing exchange and is shared after meetings. A public relief chart stays in a western bay. Restricted route records are held elsewhere under community practice, not hidden behind a secret panel in this hall.',
'Surface traders offer useful medicine and fittings in exchange for exclusive chart rights. Madger initially thinks a shared copy is a neutral compromise. Namu asks who would enforce its limits after the traders leave. The negotiation must find a service the visitors can contribute without converting hospitality into permanent ownership.',
'A child corrects Toma’s pronunciation of a harbor name. He asks what it means, expecting one neat answer. Two elders disagree and then laugh. The scene establishes a culture with internal variation before outsiders attempt to describe it as a single mysterious people.',
['Public relief chart table','Movable hearing benches','Rotation-of-speakers board','Shared harvest trays'],
'Pale limestone, deep teal textiles, mineral window light, amber lamps and violet fungal accents. Habitable dry hall above the subterranean sea, no underwater breathing or ocean inside the floor.'),
]
data={'edition':'3.1.0','title':'MADGER - Living Places','date':'2026-10-21','scope':'Thirteen furnished landmark ground floors, local histories and story opportunities within the existing fourteen-community world. Two complete companion stories deepen relationships. This additive volume does not replace the world atlas.','base_commit':'0299a6f408ac43bf98789a1cb5b3c70af2249450','chronology_rule':'Local history additions are writer canon. Scene seeds and the two short stories are authored possible episodes; their placement is provisional until a dated episode ledger adopts them. They do not silently change pilot knowledge.','landmarks':records}
(O/'Living_Places.json').write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print('Authored',len(records),'landmark dossiers')
